## Decisions

**Upgrade does NOT call init's helpers directly.** The spec says "reuses
init's helpers but bypasses their skip-if-exists guard." After reading the
init helpers, their skip-if-exists logic is deeply interleaved with their
write logic (the existence check is the first branch in each function, and
the write path is the else branch). Adding an `overwrite` flag to each
helper would change their signatures and risk breaking init's idempotency
invariant. Instead, upgrade reuses init's *content infrastructure*
(`readCanonicalDoc`, `listMethodologyDocs`, `composeStub`, `COMMANDS`) and
builds its own compare-and-write flow via `compareFile` and `spliceStub`.
This keeps init's helpers untouched and gives upgrade a clean code path
optimized for byte comparison + conditional write.

**composeStub is exported rather than duplicated.** The pointer stub
template and marker logic live in `writeMethodology`. Rather than copying
the template into upgrade (violating single source of truth), we export
`composeStub` so upgrade can generate the canonical stub content for
comparison. The export is additive — no existing consumer is affected.

**COMMANDS is exported rather than duplicated.** Same rationale. The
command registry is the single source of truth for which files belong to
the command surface. Upgrade imports it rather than maintaining a parallel
list.

**MCP config comparison is inline, not via compareFile.** The MCP config
file contains other servers that must not be disturbed. A whole-file
comparison would flag the file as "would update" any time any non-aide
server entry changed. Instead, upgrade reads the JSON, inspects only the
`aide` / `aidemd-mcp` key, and compares that key's value against the
canonical shape. This keeps the upgrade surface honest — it only reports
MCP as changed when the aide entry itself has drifted.

**IDE config uses the same additive pattern as init.** Zed config is a
JSON merge (check for `*.aide` in `file_types.Markdown`), VS Code is an
extension install check. Both are inherently idempotent, so upgrade can
re-apply them unconditionally on confirm without risk. The dry-run still
reports their status accurately by checking current state.

**Single `write` boolean gates the entire surface.** The spec mandates
all-or-nothing — no per-file opt-out. The `confirm` flag on the tool input
maps directly to the `write` parameter threaded through every comparison
call. This is the simplest possible implementation of the write gate and
eliminates any possibility of partial upgrades.

**UpgradeStepResult is a separate type from InitStepResult.** The status
vocabularies are disjoint: init uses `created | exists | installed | wired
| skipped`, upgrade uses `would update | unchanged | would create | updated
| created`. Sharing a type would require a union of all statuses, which
would weaken type safety — a caller expecting upgrade results could
accidentally receive an init-shaped status. Separate types keep each tool's
contract precise.
